"Journal of Central Asian History (https://brill.com/view/journals/jcah/4/1/article-p1_1.pdf)\nciteturn282search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: last year; \n22\nBrophy\nWanji not as a rapacious enslaver of local Ismailis, but rather as their patron, \nand someone who took up arms to defend them.57 That might imply that his \nhostility towards the rulers of Ghund had something to do with their persecu­\ntion of Ismailis.\nQing sources simply do not give us enough information to resolve this \nquestion. While noting the issue of Islamic law, Yarkand officials were more \nconcerned that the practice of slave-raiding contradicted Qing law, since all \ninhabitants of Shughnan were now “slaves of the great lord.” The Qianlong \nemperor commented that since the Tarim Basin had so flourished under his \nrule, there should be no labour shortage there, and hence no need for Muslims \nto import slaves. Eventually, a series of violent incidents involving slaves and \nslave-traders along the routes in and out of Xinjiang highlighted the disruptive \npotential of the commerce, and from 1779 onwards the emperor issued a series \nof decrees banning local Muslims from exiting the Tarim Basin to purchase \nhuman beings.58\nWhat had been a relatively lenient stance towards Shah Wanji’s expansion \ninto Ghund finally came to an end in 1778, in a distinct hardening of opin­\nions towards the Shughnan mir. A change of personnel in Yarkand may have \nbeen responsible for the shift, with newly appointed minister superintendent \nGao-pu 高樸 arriving in Yarkand in 1777. The Manchu author Cišii, who was \nserving in Kucha at this time, offers a further clue, relating that in 1776 Shah \nWanji imprisoned a trading mission that had been dispatched by Yarkand \nGovernor Hadi Beg.59 The rest of Cišii’s anecdote – in which the high-handed \nShah Wanji executes members of his own family to demonstrate his resolve – \nmay well be apocryphal, but Gao-pu’s memorials confirm that the detention \nof caravans was indeed one of Shah Wanji’s perceived misdeeds.60 By 1778 an \nescalating war of words with Qing officials is said to have reached the point of \nShah Wanji threatening to encourage the Khan of Kokand to summon an army \nand invade Kashgar.\n57\t\t\nShah, “Istoriia Shugnana”: 8; Beben, “Religious Identity”: 134–5.\n58\t\t\nQXMD vol. 105: 394–404 Memorial from Kicengge QL37/3/10 (12 April 1772). The first \nimperial decree is cited in QXMD vol. 140: 340–41. Later decrees are discussed in Laura \nNewby, “Bondage on Qing China’s Northwestern Frontier.” Modern Asian Studies 47/3 \n(2013).\n59\t\t\nCišii 七十一, Xiyu wenjian lu 西域聞見錄: juan 3, fol. 13b–14b (“Sha-guan-ji 沙關記”).\n60\t\t\nQXMD vol. 136: 36 Memorial from Gao-pu QL43/7/1 (22 August 1778). On the involvement \nof Muslim governors in regional trade, see Onuma Takahiro, “Political Power and Caravan \nMerchants at the Oasis Towns in Central Asia: The Case of Altishahr in the 17th and 18th \nCenturies.” In Xinjiang in the Context of Central Eurasian Transformations, ed. Onuma \nTakahiro, David Brophy, and Shinmen Yasushi (Tokyo: Toyo Bunko, 2018).\nJournal of Central Asian History 4 (2025) 1–30--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTHE ROAD TO SOVIET POWER 33 (https://pahar.in/pahar/Books%20and%20Articles/Central%20Asia/2007%20Birth%20of%20Tajikistan--National%20Identity%20and%20Origins%20of%20Republic%20by%20Bergne%20s.pdf)\nciteturn282search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.2 years ago; \nTHE ROAD TO SOVIET POWER 33\n\nAs already indicated, the Eastern and Western Pamirs had followed different historical paths, dictated largely by a quirk of geography which made the eastern part more accessible from the north than from the west. The eastern region, on the high plateau around Murghab⁹ was thinly populated by semi-nomadic Kyrgyz and had been annexed to the Khanate of Kokand in 1776 by the then Khan Narbuta Bii. The western part, consisting of the “volost’s” (districts) of Rushan, Shughnan, Ishkashim and Wakhan,¹⁰ had been more vulnerable to the depredations of aggressive and greedy neighbours to the west. The last and most vicious of these had been Abdul Rahman Khan, ruler of Afghanistan, who, as already described, twice laid the region waste, in 1883 and again in 1887. After the Anglo-Russian frontier settlement of 1895, both parts had been allocated to Russia. When the Khanate of Kokand was dissolved in 1876, its territory was incorporated into the Turkestan Governorate-General, and thus, after the revolution, into the Ferghana Oblast’ of the TASSR. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCanato de Cocande (https://pt.wikipedia.org/wiki/Canato_de_Cocande)\nciteturn282search14 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 9 months ago; Nela se relata que uma assembleia de anciãos e nobres de Jancate, Pilancã, Tufantipe, Partaque, Tepa-Curgã, Jainar e outras cidades elegeram o inteligente e generoso Xaruque Cã. ... Depois da destruição do Império Zungar pelos chineses em 1758, os chineses avançaram até Fergana e exigiram que Irdana Begue se tornasse vassalo do imperador Qing Qianlong. ... Numa carta enviada para a Rússia pelo qush-begi (literalmente: \"chefe dos pássaros\" ou comandante dos falcoeiros) de Tasquente, este conta que Madali tinha subjugado as cidades de Ura-tepe, Karategin (vale de Raste [Rasht], atualmente no centro do Tajiquistão), Kulab, Darqwaz  e muitas outras cidades e povos. ... O seu harém foi levado para Bucara em 40 arabas e Nasrulá convocou um grande conselho para julgar Madali, onde anunciou que tinha intenção de matar o cã deposto, apropriar-se do canato e nele colocar um governador, o que deixou os nobres conspiradores desiludidos. ... *  Não confundir esta Turquestão, atualmente uma cidade do Usbequistão, antigamente chamada Iasi, com a região histórica homónima da qual faz parte.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe History of 